          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Synthetic guarded domain theory
            (SGDT), is a field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic mathematics
            that provides an alternative to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+domain+theory"} synthetic domain theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/guarded+recursion"} guarded recursive
            definitions have (guarded)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
          %p
            More generally, the aim of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+domain+theory"} synthetic domain theory
            is roughly that computability should be built in the logic. As a result, constructions on domains would be set-theoretic with no extra structure and proofs of continuity are for free. However, such a theory would yield an unrestricted fixed-point combinator at all types which would render the theory itself inconsistent when viewed as a logical system.
          %p
            SGDT solves this problem by introducing a notion of “time”. For a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright A
            is the type of computations available one step from now. The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            modality (pronounced “later”) yields a restricted fixed-point operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext fix
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{fix} : (\triangleright A \to A) \to A
          %p
            at all types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6b%27s+theorem"} Löb's theorem
          %h2#a_model_of_sgdt A model of SGDT
          %p
            The original model of (single-clock) SGDT is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+of+trees"} topos of trees
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mtext op
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X : \omega^{\text{op}} \to \text{Set}
            which have the following shape
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mn 1
                  %mi …
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi n
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(0) \xleftarrow{r_0} X(1)\xleftarrow{r_1}  \dots \xleftarrow{r_{n-1}} X(n)\xleftarrow{r_{n}} \dots
          %p
            where the maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mtext op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \omega^{\text{op}}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The later modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            is an endofunctor in this category defined as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright A (0) = 1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▹
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright A (n+1) = A(n)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there exists an obvious map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext next
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{next} : X\to \triangleright X
            witnessing the fact that everything that is available now is also available later.
          %p
            Inspired by terminology used in the metric spaces (see below), every map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to X
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mtext Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ω
                        %mtext op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Set}^{\omega^\text{op}}
            is called
            %em non-expansive
            while a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to X
            is
            %em contractive
            if it factors through the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext next
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{next}
            map, i.e. there exists a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mo ▹
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : \triangleright X \to X
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mtext next
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = g \circ \text{next}
          %h3#fixedpoint_construction  Fixed-point construction
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▹
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, f)
            yields a natural transformation with components
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0 : 1 \to X(0)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i : X(i) \to X(i+1)
            commuting with the restriction maps of the object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-chain" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \xrightarrow{f_0} X(0) \xrightarrow{f_1} X(1)  \dots \to X(n) \dots
          %p
            The morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mtext fix
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{fix}_f : 1 \to X
            is itself a natural transformation whose components are
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi …
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0 \circ f_1 \circ \dots f_{i-1} \circ f_{i} : 1 \to X(i)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mtext op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \omega^{\text{op}}
            satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mtext fix
                    %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mtext next
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mtext fix
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{fix}_f = f \circ \text{next} \circ (\text{fix}_f)
          %p
            The idea here is that the recursive call is
            %strong guarded
            by the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext next
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{next}
            map.
          %h3#relation_with_metric_spaces Relation with metric spaces
          %p
            The category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
            bisected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} ultrametric spaces
            (BiCUlt) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
            of the topos of trees (
            %a{:href => "#BMSS2012"}> Proposition 5.1
            ).
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext BiCUlt
                  %munderover
                    %mo ⊥
                    %munder
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ι
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{BiCUlt}
                  \\underoverset
                  {\underset{}{\leftarrow}}{\overset{\iota}{\hookrightarrow}}{\bot }
                  \\mathcal{S}
          %p
            Moreover, the inclusion functor restricts to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            when considering objects in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            whose restriction maps are surjective, which are sometimes called the total objects or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flabby+sheaves"} flabby sheaves
          %p
            First, recall that every point in a ball
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            of an ultrametric space is at its centre while intersecting balls are contained into each other which means that closed
            = succeed "-balls" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{-n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, d)
            by defining a family of equivalence relation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x =_n y \Leftrightarrow d(x,y) \le 2^{-n}
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GianantonioM03"} (Di Gianantonio and Miculan, 2003)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are equal at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            if they belong to the same
            = succeed "-ball." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{-n}
          %p
            The inclusion functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ι
                  %mo :
                  %mtext BiCUlt
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota: \text{BiCUlt} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{S}
            takes a metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,d)
            in CBUlt into the presheaf of its space decompositions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi …
                  %mo ←
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X/=_1) \leftarrow (X/=_2) \leftarrow  \dots \leftarrow (X/=_n) \leftarrow \dots
          %p
            where each restriction map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X/=_{(n+1)}) \to (X/=_{n})
            takes a
            = succeed "-ball" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{-(n+1)}
            = succeed "-ball" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{-n}
          %p
            The inclusion functor has a right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo →
                  %mtext BiCUlt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S} \to \text{BiCUlt}
            which maps a presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the homset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(1, X)
            (the global elements of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            when seen as a diagram in
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}(1, X)
            is defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi i
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) =  \bigsqcap \{ 2^{-n} \mid \forall i \lt n . x_j(\star)  = y_j(\star)  \}
          %p
            for natural transformations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y : 1 \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            agree.
          %p
            A summary of these results can be found in Bizjak’s Ph.D. thesis (
            %a{:href => "#Bizjak16"}> Bizjak 16, Section 1.2
            ).
          %h2#axiomatics Axiomatics
          %p
            %a{:href => "#PalombiSterling22"} Palombi and Sterling
            provide the following simple axiomatization of a model of (single-clock) synthetic guarded domain theory:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %li
              %p
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact"} left exact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ▹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright
            %li
              %p
                with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mtext next
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mo ▹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\text{next}
                      \\;\colon\; 1 \to \triangleright
                satisfying
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ▹
                      %mtext next
                      %mo =
                      %mtext next
                      %mo ▹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleright\text{next} = \text{next}\triangleright
            %li
              %p
                supporting
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%B6b%27s+theorem"} Löb-induction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ▹
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \Omega|\triangleright \phi \Rightarrow \phi \vdash \phi
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            Then “multi-clock” SGDT is simply a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            with an object of clocks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \colon E
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/K
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of single-clock SGDT.
          %h2#type_theory Type Theory
          %p
            Mathematics in synthetic guarded domain theory can be formalized in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of SGDT models. Several presentations have been proposed such as:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em guarded dependent type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "#BGCMB2016"}> BGCMB2016
                ) which is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            %li
